question_id|category|question|have_it|what_we_have|how_to_get_it|why_it_matters|official_source_url
A10-Q01|Prices|What were the opening and closing numbers on every market we originate?|no||Licence an official or commercially-licensed odds feed, or subscribe to a price-archiving service that republishes sportsbook opening/closing prices with timestamps. Alternatively, capture prices in-house at two fixed clock times per week from a licensed operator's public pages and store the raw HTML as the evidence of the capture.|Blocks ROI entirely. Without it no strategy can leave paper, and the leaderboard can only be ranked on probability scoring.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-10
A10-Q02|Prices|At what time is 'opening' defined for a market that opens on Sunday but is posted the previous Wednesday?|no||Pick a rule and pre-register it before any bet is placed: e.g. opening = first posted price for the market; closing = price at kickoff. Write the rule into the store schema so it cannot be re-litigated after the fact.|Without a pre-registered definition, closing-line value can be gamed by choosing whichever snapshot is convenient.|https://www.nfl.com/schedules/2026/REG10
A10-Q03|Prices|Are we consistently getting the better of the closing number (closing-line value)?|no||Requires Q01 first. Then compute mean and median CLV over a pre-registered number of bets; CLV is a far faster read on skill than ROI because it does not need results to converge.|CLV is the single best early-warning signal that our origination is beating the market.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-10
A10-Q04|Results|What were the final scores of 2026 week 4?|no|16 games missing; only NE 29 @ BUF 26 (Oct 4) is known|Re-fetch https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-4 - the page returns the placeholder string 'Hidden Score' in place of scores on some renders, so retry until the scores render. Fall back to https://www.nfl.com/schedules/2026/by-team/<club-slug> per club, which carries each club's completed game log.|The results feed is 120 of ~136 games. Training on a season with a hole in it biases the ratings for the 32 clubs involved in week 4.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-4
A10-Q05|Results|Are the standings derived from our captured results consistent with the published standings?|partial|Checked club by club; see the completeness table|Re-run the PF/PA reconciliation after week 4 is recovered. The check is exact: sum of captured points for and against each club must equal the PF/PA on nfl.com/standings.|This is the control that proves the results feed was transcribed correctly and is complete.|https://www.nfl.com/standings/league/2026/REG
A10-Q06|Results|Which games in the feed were affected by weather, overtime or a mid-game quarterback injury?|no||Not published as a structured field on the scores pages used. Would need box scores or play-by-play, both of which are outside what this session captured.|Extreme-context games inflate the residual variance and make the model look worse calibrated than it is.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-9
A10-Q07|Origination|Is a least-squares margin model the right functional form, or should it be a logistic/gradient-boosted model on drive-level data?|partial|Compared against a constant baseline only|Benchmark the current model against two challengers on the same walk-forward splits, and only adopt a challenger that wins on out-of-sample log-loss, not on in-sample fit.|Choice of model determines the ceiling on every downstream signal.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-1
A10-Q08|Origination|Should the home-field advantage be fitted globally or per-venue?|partial|Fitted globally; the 2026 estimate came out slightly negative, which is unusual and may be a small-sample artefact|Re-fit with venue fixed effects once the sample is larger, and pre-register a sign constraint only if there is a documented reason to expect one.|A negative HFA moves every originated spread by the same amount, so a wrong sign is a systematic, not random, error.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-1
A10-Q09|Origination|How much of the rating is real and how much is noise at this sample size?|partial|Split-season margin stability is low|Shrink the ratings toward the mean using the observed split-season correlation as the shrinkage target, and re-measure the stability correlation each week.|Unshrunk ratings over-fit small samples and produce confident bets on teams that simply got lucky.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-1
A10-Q10|Depth|For each player ruled Out, which string replaces them and how deep does the club have to reach?|partial|Resolved only for the clubs whose depth chart rendered as text (GB, SEA, MIN, DAL). The other 24 clubs are unresolved.|Fetch https://www.<club>.com/team/depth-chart for all 28 clubs on the Week 10 report. Note the Houston Texans render their depth chart as an image, so an alternative official route is needed there. Automate the capture weekly - the charts change.|The depth fade strategy produced only 2 signals because the depth weights are zero for 24 of 28 clubs. The signal cannot be evaluated until coverage is complete.|https://www.nfl.com/injuries/
A10-Q11|Depth|How much is a 2nd-string replacement worth versus a 3rd or 4th, in points?|no||This is an empirical question and needs a multi-season dataset joining depth charts to results. The weights published in this pack are PROPOSED (labelled proposed_weight_not_observed) and must not be used to price anything until fitted.|The depth fade is the most promising original signal in this pack and it currently has no calibrated weights.|https://www.nfl.com/injuries/
A10-Q12|Depth|Do the Week 10 injury report rosters match reality?|partial|Several listed players do not correspond to the clubs this session would expect from prior seasons|Cross-check each club's injury report against its official club-site roster page. Flag any player who appears on the report but not on the roster.|If the report rosters are synthetic or stale, everything derived from them is suspect.|https://www.nfl.com/injuries/
A10-Q13|Staking|What is the bankroll, and what fraction of it may a single bet risk?|no||A human decision, not a data question. It must be answered before any live staking plan is armed; the demotion gate references it.|Determines whether the staking layer is even meaningful.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-10
A10-Q14|Staking|Should staking be capped independently of Kelly?|no||Recommendation: yes, cap at a fixed fraction of bankroll. See suggestion A10-S05.|Full Kelly assumes the probability estimates are exact; ours are not, so uncapped Kelly over-bets.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-1
A10-Q15|Correlation|What is the true correlation between the side and total residuals, and is it stable?|partial|Measured in-sample on 120 games at r = -0.18 with a 95% CI that includes zero|Re-measure out-of-sample, on residuals from the walk-forward fits rather than in-sample residuals, and only once there are more games.|Same-game parlays are priced off this number. If it is really zero the independent product is correct; if it is not, parlays are mispriced in one direction.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-1
A10-Q16|Parlays|Do sportsbooks price our correlated parlays worse or better than the independent product?|no||Requires book prices for parlay markets. Not on any official NFL page; needs the same licensed feed as Q01.|Determines whether the parlay family is worth building at all.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-10
A10-Q17|Props|Is there any official source of player-prop lines or usage data?|no||None found on nfl.com this session. Prop pricing needs either a licensed feed or play-by-play data, which nextgenstats.nfl.com publishes for some metrics - that is the route to try first.|Blocks the entire player-prop-combo family; A9-S10 is registered but unbuilt because of this.|https://nextgenstats.nfl.com/
A10-Q18|Governance|Who owns the decision to promote a strategy from paper to live?|no||Name an owner in COORDINATION.md and make the promotion a recorded event, not an implicit one.|Without a named owner, the demotion gate is unenforceable.|https://www.nfl.com/scores/2026/week-10
